---
import { existsSync } from "node:fs";
import { join } from "node:path";

import Issue from "../../layouts/Issue.astro";
import Masthead from "../../components/Masthead.astro";
import HeroThesis from "../../components/HeroThesis.astro";
import AudioBrief from "../../components/AudioBrief.astro";
import Synthesis from "../../components/Synthesis.astro";
import Signal from "../../components/Signal.astro";
import ClusterCallout from "../../components/ClusterCallout.astro";
import Methodology from "../../components/Methodology.astro";
import previousIssues from "../../data/issues.json";

const issueDate = "2026-06-08";
const description =
  "US AI governance split in one week between a federal voluntary shadow framework and the first state mandatory safety audit law, as the industry's own PAC was caught astroturfing the debate.";
const title = "The Cinnabar Dispatch · 2026-06-08 · Possibilities with Probabilities";

// Audio brief is graceful: renders if MP3 exists at the expected path, else nothing.
// Path is checked at build time against the public/ directory.
const audioFilename = `${issueDate}.mp3`;
const audioFsPath = join(process.cwd(), "public", "audio", audioFilename);
const audioPath = existsSync(audioFsPath)
  ? `${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}audio/${audioFilename}`
  : null;

const filteredIssues = previousIssues.filter((i) => i.date !== issueDate);
---

<Issue title={title} description={description} issueDate={issueDate}>
  <Masthead
    issueDate={issueDate}
    scanNumber={9}
    signalCount={7}
    strongCount={4}
    weakCount={3}
  />

  <HeroThesis kicker="This week's thesis">
    Within seventy-two hours, the US produced two incompatible AI governance
    architectures: a White House executive order creating a classified,
    voluntary federal framework with NSA oversight and secret criteria, and
    Illinois SB 315 passing its legislature unanimously to become the first US
    law mandating annual independent safety audits for frontier AI labs. In the
    same week, the political action committee funded by OpenAI resources was
    confirmed running fake accounts impersonating AI critics, and Bain Capital
    reported that forty-four percent of large companies are funding their next
    AI wave on savings that have not yet materialized. The governance debate is
    now contested both formally, between two divergent US regulatory
    philosophies, and informally, by the industry's own political apparatus.
  </HeroThesis>

  <AudioBrief audioPath={audioPath} />

  <Synthesis>
    <p>
      This week's scan produced seven signals across three domains, with a
      structural collision at the center. The Trump administration's executive
      order on frontier AI models (June 2) created a federal preflight-check
      framework: labs may voluntarily provide the NSA up to thirty days of
      pre-release model access for classified capability benchmarking, with
      criteria that remain secret and no mandatory requirements. Illinois SB 315
      cleared its legislature 110 to 0 in the House and 52 to 5 in the Senate
      within the same seventy-two hours, with Governor Pritzker signaling his
      intent to sign into the first US law requiring annual independent
      third-party safety audits for frontier AI developers, plus seventy-two-hour
      incident reporting and civil penalties up to three million dollars per
      incident. Build American AI, the PAC funded by OpenAI resources and
      personally by OpenAI President Greg Brockman, confirmed in the same week
      that outside vendors it hired ran fake accounts impersonating AI critics,
      including accounts posting provocative imagery. Bain Capital's research,
      cited in Zvi Mowshowitz's June 4 roundup, found that forty-four percent of
      large companies are funding next-wave AI investment on AI-driven cost
      savings that have not yet materialized. Microsoft launched MAI-Thinking-1,
      a one-trillion-parameter mixture-of-experts reasoning model, as the first
      proprietary frontier-class entry from a company outside the existing
      four-lab oligopoly.
    </p>

    <p>
      The structural observation binding signals one, two, and three is
      not simply that governance is accelerating but that the two US governance
      tracks now point in opposite directions. The Trump EO is federal,
      voluntary, and classified: it rewards labs for participation with
      partner-selection power while keeping the criteria that define "covered
      frontier model" invisible to the public and to enterprise buyers. Illinois
      SB 315 is state, mandatory, and public: it imposes independent audits,
      whistleblower protections, and attorney-general enforcement with published
      results. Both were passed in the same week by institutions that endorsed
      the same frontier labs.
      <strong>The implication for enterprise AI strategy is not that governance
      is coming; it is that two governance philosophies, one that rewards
      opacity and one that mandates transparency, are now simultaneously
      operative in the US market, and enterprises will need to design
      vendor-risk frameworks that can accommodate both.</strong>
      The Build American AI confirmation adds a third dimension: the regulatory
      debate itself is now susceptible to coordinated inauthentic influence from
      the industry it is trying to govern.
    </p>

    <p>
      For the reader whose work is enterprise AI strategy: the Illinois SB 315
      passage creates a new vendor due-diligence requirement that did not exist
      last week: whether your frontier AI provider has completed or is planning
      an independent safety audit under a US mandatory framework. The EU AI Act
      Omnibus delay (logged May 29 scan) removed compliance pressure from one
      direction; SB 315 restores it from another, and with a shorter timeline.
      The Bain forty-four percent finding is the more immediate planning risk: if
      nearly half of your peer companies are committing expanded AI spend on
      savings that have not arrived, the inflection point where budget
      contractions arrive simultaneously is a scenario worth stress-testing now.
      And the Trump EO's voluntary structure offers a practical asymmetry to
      watch: labs that participate in the classified preflight window gain
      partner-selection power that labs outside the program will not have, which
      may affect procurement options in regulated federal-adjacent sectors within
      the next twelve months.
    </p>
  </Synthesis>

  <Signal
    index={1}
    date="2026-06-02"
    category="04 AI Governance & Ethics"
    title="Trump executive order establishes classified preflight-check framework for frontier AI models; NSA designated oversight authority with secret criteria."
    profile="E4 T-Shifting U2 H-Grounded Z-Now"
    strength="strong"
    logic={{
      what: "US executive order created a voluntary NSA preflight-check window for frontier AI models.",
      mechanism: "Secret benchmarking criteria create information asymmetry; early-participant labs gain partner-selection power enterprises cannot audit.",
      soWhat: "Enterprise vendor selection in defense, healthcare, and finance now partly turns on undisclosed government-relationship depth.",
    }}
    sources={[
      {
        label: "whitehouse.gov/presidential-actions/2026/06/promoting-advanced-artificial-intelligence-innovation-and-security",
        url: "https://www.whitehouse.gov/presidential-actions/2026/06/promoting-advanced-artificial-intelligence-innovation-and-security/",
      },
      {
        label: "crowell.com",
        url: "https://www.crowell.com/en/insights/client-alerts/executive-order-creates-voluntary-regulatory-regime-of-frontier-ai-models",
      },
      {
        label: "thezvi.substack.com/p/ai-171-false-flag",
        url: "https://thezvi.substack.com/p/ai-171-false-flag",
      },
    ]}
  >
    <p slot="why">
      President Trump signed the first US federal mechanism for pre-deployment
      frontier AI model review: developers may voluntarily provide the government
      up to thirty days of pre-release access to covered frontier models for
      classified NSA-led benchmarking of advanced cyber capabilities. Within
      sixty days, agencies must develop a classified benchmarking process to
      determine what qualifies as a covered frontier model, with the criteria
      remaining secret. Labs that participate help select trusted partners for
      early model access, creating a first-mover advantage for compliant
      companies. The order is nominally voluntary and explicitly bars mandatory
      pre-clearance requirements; the practical effect is a new
      government-industry checkpoint at federal level that did not exist before
      June 2026. For enterprise AI procurement, this begins differentiating
      frontier labs by government-relationship depth, a factor likely to influence
      vendor selection in regulated sectors including defense, healthcare, and
      finance. Multiple legal analyses note a structural tension: the opacity of
      the benchmarking criteria makes it impossible for enterprises to assess
      which labs are participating or on what terms, creating an information
      asymmetry that benefits early participants.
    </p>
    <p slot="watch">
      Whether any frontier lab publicly confirms participation in the thirty-day
      preflight window, which would establish it as a de facto norm even without
      mandatory status. Whether the classified benchmarking criteria eventually
      surface through Congressional oversight hearings, providing enterprises
      with visibility into the thresholds the NSA is applying and the
      participation list of labs that have engaged.
    </p>
  </Signal>

  <Signal
    index={2}
    date="2026-05-28"
    category="04 AI Governance & Ethics"
    title="Illinois SB 315 passes legislature 110 to 0 and 52 to 5; first US law mandating annual independent safety audits for frontier AI labs, with civil penalties and incident reporting."
    profile="E4 T-Shifting U3 H-Grounded Z-Now"
    strength="strong"
    logic={{
      what: "Illinois SB 315 cleared both chambers near-unanimously, mandating annual independent frontier-AI safety audits with civil penalties.",
      mechanism: "Unlike prior self-attestation frameworks, SB 315 requires ongoing independent third-party verification with named penalties and public enforcement.",
      soWhat: "Enterprise vendor due diligence now requires confirming whether frontier providers have completed a mandatory independent safety audit.",
    }}
    sources={[
      {
        label: "capitolnewsillinois.com",
        url: "https://capitolnewsillinois.com/news/illinois-lawmakers-pass-landmark-ai-accountability-bill/",
      },
      {
        label: "nbcnews.com/tech/tech-news/illinois-legislature-passes-historic-ai-bill-rcna347191",
        url: "https://www.nbcnews.com/tech/tech-news/illinois-legislature-passes-historic-ai-bill-rcna347191",
      },
      {
        label: "iapp.org",
        url: "https://iapp.org/news/a/notable-ai-privacy-bills-hit-finish-line-in-illinois-connecticut-and-new-york",
      },
    ]}
  >
    <p slot="why">
      Illinois SB 315 cleared both chambers with near-unanimous margins: one
      hundred and ten to zero in the House, fifty-two to five in the Senate, with
      Governor Pritzker publicly indicating his intent to sign. The bill requires
      frontier AI developers to publish and annually update a safety framework
      covering catastrophic-risk assessment, cybersecurity, internal governance,
      and third-party evaluations; to report serious AI failures within
      seventy-two hours; to protect employee whistleblowers; and to face civil
      penalties up to three million dollars per incident enforced by the Illinois
      attorney general. OpenAI and Anthropic supported the bill; other AI trade
      groups opposed it. This is structurally different from prior state AI
      activity such as California watermarking requirements or Colorado risk
      assessments: it targets the frontier lab directly, requires ongoing
      independent verification rather than self-attestation, and creates
      enforcement mechanisms with named penalties. For enterprise AI procurement,
      this creates a new vendor due-diligence requirement that did not exist last
      week: whether your frontier AI provider has completed, or is planning, an
      independent safety audit under a US mandatory framework.
    </p>
    <p slot="watch">
      Whether Governor Pritzker signs, converting the bill to enacted law. Whether
      a second enterprise-heavy state (Texas, New York, California) introduces
      comparable frontier-lab audit legislation in its next legislative session.
      Two-state replication would create a de facto compliance norm for US-based
      frontier labs regardless of federal preemption status.
    </p>
  </Signal>

  <Signal
    index={3}
    date="2026-06-04"
    category="04 AI Governance & Ethics"
    title="OpenAI-backed PAC confirmed running fake accounts impersonating AI critics; first AI-lab-affiliated political influence operation targeting AI governance discourse."
    profile="E3 T-Emerging U2 H-Grounded Z-Now"
    strength="strong"
    logic={{
      what: "The OpenAI-backed PAC confirmed outside vendors ran fake social media accounts impersonating AI critics and governance skeptics.",
      mechanism: "This is the first confirmed AI-lab-affiliated influence operation targeting the governance debate those same labs face.",
      soWhat: "The governance debate is now susceptible to AI-funded manipulation, compounding the trust deficit between regulators and frontier labs.",
    }}
    sources={[
      {
        label: "thezvi.substack.com/p/ai-171-false-flag",
        url: "https://thezvi.substack.com/p/ai-171-false-flag",
      },
    ]}
  >
    <p slot="why">
      Build American AI, a political action committee funded by OpenAI resources
      and personally by OpenAI President Greg Brockman, confirmed that outside
      vendors it hired ran fake social media accounts impersonating AI skeptics,
      including accounts posting provocative imagery. The PAC characterized these
      as parody accounts run by outside vendors and not a core part of its
      strategy. OpenAI issued a public statement saying no outside political group
      speaks for OpenAI and explicitly criticizing astroturfing tactics.
      Congressional staff reportedly view the PAC as OpenAI's PAC regardless of
      the official distancing. This is the first confirmed case of a frontier-lab
      affiliated entity using coordinated inauthentic political influence
      operations to shape AI governance discourse, at the precise moment that
      political bodies are debating mandatory AI regulation. The structural
      consequence is a meta-governance problem: the regulatory debate over AI is
      now susceptible to AI-funded manipulation, which compounds the trust deficit
      that the SB 315 and Trump EO signals represent. OpenAI simultaneously
      publicly supported SB 315 and the Trump EO's voluntary compliance model,
      creating an accountability gap legislators have noted explicitly.
    </p>
    <p slot="watch">
      Whether the FEC or any state attorney general opens enforcement
      investigations into Build American AI's activities, establishing legal
      precedent for AI company PAC accountability. Whether a Congressional
      committee adds political influence disclosure requirements to proposed
      federal AI legislation as a direct response to this incident.
    </p>
  </Signal>

  <Signal
    index={4}
    date="2026-06-04"
    category="02 Enterprise AI & Org Transformation"
    title="Bain Capital finds forty-four percent of large companies funding next-wave AI investment from savings that have not yet materialized; circular ROI risk flagged."
    profile="E3 T-Emerging U2 H-Grounded Z-Now"
    strength="strong"
    logic={{
      what: "Bain Capital found forty-four percent of large companies funding next-wave AI investment on savings not yet realized.",
      mechanism: "Organizations committing expanded AI spend on projected but unrealized savings create circular financing risk, not financial discipline.",
      soWhat: "If projected savings fail to arrive, simultaneous budget contractions across the large-company cohort are a scenario to stress-test.",
    }}
    sources={[
      {
        label: "thezvi.substack.com/p/ai-171-false-flag",
        url: "https://thezvi.substack.com/p/ai-171-false-flag",
      },
    ]}
  >
    <p slot="why">
      Bain Capital's research finds that forty-four percent of large companies
      are funding the next wave of AI investment based on AI-driven cost savings
      that have not yet been realized, which Bain characterizes as circular risk
      rather than financial discipline: organizations are committing to expanded
      AI spend before current investments have produced the reductions that were
      supposed to justify them. This sits in direct tension with the supply-side
      acceleration signals from this same week, including one company reportedly
      spending five hundred million dollars on Claude in a single month and
      Anthropic's annualized revenue reaching forty-seven billion dollars in May
      2026. The revenue acceleration visible at the frontier-lab supply side is
      being partially financed by projected demand-side savings that have not
      arrived. Enterprise AI budget frameworks were designed for deterministic
      software consumption patterns; agentic systems consume tokens proportional
      to autonomous task volume, which grows exponentially within budgets designed
      for linear growth. The Bain finding extends the April enterprise token cost
      crisis signal (Uber, Microsoft) from individual incidents to a structural
      financing pattern across forty-four percent of the large-company cohort.
    </p>
    <p slot="watch">
      Whether a major analyst firm (Gartner, Forrester, IDC) or consultancy
      (Accenture, McKinsey, Deloitte) publishes enterprise AI ROI-realization
      data for the S&P 500 in Q3-Q4 2026, providing independent corroboration or
      refutation of the forty-four percent figure. Whether any Fortune 500 company
      publicly reports an AI program restructuring citing savings shortfall within
      the next two quarters, converting the structural risk from statistical to
      named and public.
    </p>
  </Signal>

  <Signal
    index={5}
    date="2026-06-02"
    category="01 GenAI Capabilities"
    title="Microsoft launches MAI-Thinking-1, a one-trillion-parameter reasoning model; first proprietary frontier competitor outside the existing four-lab oligopoly."
    profile="E2 T-Emerging U2 H-Ahead Z-Near"
    strength="weak"
    logic={{
      what: "Microsoft launched its first in-house frontier reasoning model, entering the US frontier-lab oligopoly as a fifth competitor.",
      mechanism: "Performance claims are vendor-internal and not yet independently benchmarked (E2); the fifth-entrant structural shift is real regardless.",
      soWhat: "Azure-scale enterprise distribution and GitHub Copilot give Microsoft deployment reach that may widen procurement choices in regulated sectors.",
    }}
    sources={[
      {
        label: "simonwillison.net/2026/Jun/2/microsofts-new-models",
        url: "https://simonwillison.net/2026/Jun/2/microsofts-new-models/",
      },
    ]}
  >
    <p slot="why">
      Microsoft launched MAI-Thinking-1 (a one-trillion-parameter,
      thirty-five-billion-active-parameter mixture-of-experts reasoning model)
      and MAI-Code-1-Flash (a one-hundred-and-thirty-seven-billion-parameter,
      five-billion-active coding model), representing Microsoft's first in-house
      proprietary frontier-class models rather than distributing OpenAI or
      Anthropic output. Microsoft claims MAI-Thinking-1 is preferred to Sonnet
      4.6 in blind human side-by-side evaluations, an internal claim without
      independent benchmark replication. The structural significance is the
      competitive landscape shift: the effective frontier-model oligopoly
      (OpenAI, Anthropic, Google, Meta) now has a fifth US entrant with Azure
      distribution at enterprise scale and GitHub Copilot as an existing
      deployment channel for MAI-Code-1-Flash, rolling out to individual users
      immediately. Evidence is E2 because the performance claims are
      vendor-internal evaluations not yet independently verified; the Hype rating
      is Ahead of itself because Microsoft's evaluation methodology and comparison
      conditions are not disclosed. The signal is logged because the structural
      change (a fifth US frontier entrant with enterprise-scale distribution) is
      real regardless of whether the performance claims hold.
    </p>
    <p slot="watch">
      Whether independent benchmark organizations (HELM, LMSYS Chatbot Arena,
      LiveBench) publish evaluations placing MAI-Thinking-1 against frontier
      peers within Q3 2026, confirming or refuting Microsoft's side-by-side
      claim. Whether MAI-Code-1-Flash shows measurable acceptance-rate or
      productivity differences from the prior Copilot baseline in public GitHub
      data, providing real-world deployment evidence independent of vendor claims.
    </p>
  </Signal>

  <ClusterCallout kicker="The pattern across signals 1, 2, and 3">
    <p>
      The Trump EO (federal, voluntary, classified criteria) and Illinois SB 315
      (state, mandatory, public penalties and audits) were both enacted within
      seventy-two hours and were both publicly supported by the same frontier
      labs. They represent incompatible philosophies: one rewards opacity and
      first-mover compliance advantage, the other mandates transparency and
      independent verification. Build American AI's confirmed fake-account
      campaign arrived in the same week, making the governance debate itself an
      object of coordinated manipulation.
      <strong>Enterprises designing AI vendor-risk frameworks for 2026-2028 now
      face a US regulatory environment with two simultaneous governing
      philosophies, neither of which has precedence over the other, and an
      industry whose political credibility is contested by its own disclosed
      conduct.</strong>
    </p>
  </ClusterCallout>

  {filteredIssues.length > 0 && (
    <section class="previous-issues">
      <h2 class="previous-issues__heading">Previous Issues</h2>
      <ul class="previous-issues__list">
        {filteredIssues.map((issue) => (
          <li>
            <a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}issues/${issue.date}/`}>
              Week of {issue.date}
            </a>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </section>
  )}

  <Methodology
    frameworkUrl="https://cinnabar-intel.github.io/design/05-signal-scoring-framework/"
    archiveUrl="https://cinnabar-intel.github.io/baseline/zone2-futures-intelligence/06-weak-signal-watch/"
  />
</Issue>
